{#- Which windows a price row covers (Chase 2026-10-31: "a visual for which windows are getting
    tinted, small line drawing, simple but very intuitive"; "needs to be at a side, front, back
    viewing"; "use different cars for car suv pickup and van, also for car make it distinct which
    way is front"). One vehicle per class (car = sedan, suv, pickup = crew cab, van = minivan),
    drawn three ways left to right: SIDE, nose to the right (longer hood, a side mirror at the
    windshield, a headlight at the nose, a taillight at the tail), FRONT 3/4 (Chase: "make the front and back of the cars 3/4 shots"; the nose, headlights
    and grille, with the side going back) and REAR 3/4 (taillights and plate, the side going forward). Every glass panel is outlined and the glass in the job is
    filled solid: front doors and rear side glass (rear doors, plus the SUV's and van's rear quarter
    windows) in the side view, the windshield top strip in the front 3/4, the back glass (the
    pickup's cab glass) in the rear 3/4; the door glass also shows lit on the 3/4 views' near side, the sunroof on the side view's roof (sunroof row only).
    Decorative: the row label says the same in words, so the drawing is aria-hidden.
    Styles: assets/v3/css/55-price-row.css (.v3-win, .v3-veh).

    Import:  {% from "v3/windows.njk" import windows, vehicle %}
    Use:     {{ windows("two_front_doors", "suv") }}   any pricing.json coverage or panel key, or a
             dotted ref ending in one; class car | suv | pickup | van (default car). Unknown key: nothing.
             {{ vehicle("pickup") }}   the side outline alone, small, for a column heading. -#}

